WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.360
If I were to tell you ‘Rain World has the
most complex ecosystem of any game,’ you

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.850
might go…

00:00:12.850 --> 00:00:14.000
‘What?’

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.100
Because Rain World doesn’t seem particularly
advanced — it’s a 2D pixel art game where

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:24.210
you play as a weird slug, how complicated
could its world really be?

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:29.460
While there are other games with fancier graphics
and denser lore, Rain World’s unique system

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:35.640
of procedurally generated animation and complex
AI come together to simulate the feeling of

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:40.280
a living ecosystem better than (almost) any
other game I’ve played.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.750
So, for this entry into the archive, we’ll
uncover the fascinating intricacies of Rain

00:00:45.750 --> 00:00:47.879
World’s pixeled biosphere.

00:00:47.879 --> 00:00:55.210
Now, let’s become part of this virtual food
chain…

00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:00.690
Rain World begins with a cutscene showing
a family of weasel-like creatures called Slugcats

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.130
struggling to survive.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:08.270
A storm breaks out and a young Slugcat, the
protagonist, falls into the drains below,

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:11.480
and awakens in a new and unknown environment.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:16.090
The game then quite literally drops you into
the ecosystem.

00:01:16.090 --> 00:01:21.799
It’s disorienting at first, but pretty quickly
you learn how to live like a Slugcat.

00:01:21.799 --> 00:01:26.860
In this world, you are small but crafty, able
to catch and feed on small creatures like

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:28.610
the fluttering Batflies.

00:01:28.610 --> 00:01:32.500
But from your first steps, the animation feels…

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.500
different.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:35.790
And that’s because it is.

00:01:35.790 --> 00:01:41.119
Like most of the creatures you’ll meet in
Rainworld, the Slugcat is procedurally animated,

00:01:41.119 --> 00:01:46.700
meaning its animation is determined in real
time through code interacting with the game’s

00:01:46.700 --> 00:01:47.700
environment.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.850
Since the dawn of animation, pretty much the
only way to get a creature to move has been

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:55.040
to hand animate it moving frame by frame.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:59.979
But in Rain World, a creature’s motion happens
organically within a set of predetermined

00:01:59.979 --> 00:02:05.659
parameters, creating movement that feels uncannily
spontaneous.

00:02:05.659 --> 00:02:10.280
One mind-blowing example of Rain World’s
complexity are the Lizards.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:15.390
These enemies move with procedural animation
just like the Slugcat, able to run across

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:19.080
flat ground, crawl through tunnels, and climb
up poles.

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.830
But they take this to another level with their
AI.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.900
When one lizard catches you, it will try to
drag you back to its den.

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:32.250
But if another lizard passes by, its AI will
cause it to try and snatch you for itself,

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.160
which can give you a dynamic opportunity to
escape.

00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:39.090
But that’s just scratching the surface of
lizard complexity.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:45.090
Each lizard also has their own simulated personality
based on six unique modifies that determine

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:47.080
how they interact with each other.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:51.440
And on top of that, there are nine different
types of lizards in this game, all with their

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.550
own distinctive abilities and behaviors.

00:02:54.550 --> 00:02:59.840
For example, Yellow Lizards have long antennae
that allow them to communicate with each other,

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:05.700
meaning their AI’s work together to surround
the Slugcat or cut them off using the terrain.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.190
White Lizards can camouflage with whatever
pixels are behind them in their environment,

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:16.530
so they hunt by staying completely still until
the Slugcat gets close, then lash out and

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.820
try to pull the Slugcat in with their long
tongues.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:25.110
Cyan Lizards can use bursts of energy to pull
off incredible jumps that when paired with

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:30.129
their AI leads to some of the most hilariously
unpredictable movement patterns I’ve ever

00:03:30.129 --> 00:03:32.549
seen in a video game.

00:03:32.549 --> 00:03:36.790
And there’s another hidden system that can
go completely unnoticed by the majority of

00:03:36.790 --> 00:03:41.880
players — if you consistently feed a lizard
their preferred food, they will eventually

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:47.180
become your ally and start following you around,
and will even get into fights with other lizards

00:03:47.180 --> 00:03:48.450
that try to eat you.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:53.490
It’s a surprisingly detailed system that
shows just how meticulous Rain World is when

00:03:53.490 --> 00:03:57.629
it comes to its ecosystem.

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:02.500
In the next region of the game, far larger
predators called Vultures sometimes swoop

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:05.830
in from above on nightmarish wings.

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:10.470
Vultures will also climb after the player
and squeeze through gaps — their procedural

00:04:10.470 --> 00:04:13.189
animation particularly uncanny.

00:04:13.189 --> 00:04:18.470
While vultures are intimidating, as scavengers,
they can be scared off if you stand your ground

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:23.310
and throw spears at them, which will sometimes
cause them to drop their masks — seemingly

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:26.680
implying these predators aren’t entirely
organic.

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:31.400
If you hold a vulture’s mask in front of
you, most creatures will run from the player’s

00:04:31.400 --> 00:04:37.170
Slugcat, instinctually scared off by your
resemblance to a top predator.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:42.140
Giant orange Centipedes also lurk in the recesses
of this ruined landscape.

00:04:42.140 --> 00:04:46.070
If one catches you, they’ll admit a lethal
electric shock.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:50.610
Like clockwork, the centipedes move with a
general sense of coordination.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:56.400
Indeed, much of Rain World’s ecosystem follows
a similar pattern, with the titular rain that

00:04:56.400 --> 00:04:59.960
comes every so often marking the end of each
cycle.

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:04.479
When the rain falls, all lifeforms must hibernate
or drown in the downpour.

00:05:04.479 --> 00:05:10.120
Soon, the player settles into these cycles,
and feels like a small part of the larger

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:13.330
biosphere.

00:05:13.330 --> 00:05:18.949
Following the dripping pipes deeper underground,
the Slugcat plummets into a kingdom of waterlogged

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:22.009
tunnels known as the Drainage System.

00:05:22.009 --> 00:05:25.500
Swarms of Leeches writhe in these half-flooded
chambers.

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:30.790
When the Slugcat is close, the Leeches’
AI causes them to go into a frenzy, following

00:05:30.790 --> 00:05:34.770
the Slugcat’s movements and waiting for
the chance to latch on.

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:39.789
If the Slugcat slips, their best chance of
survival is getting back above the water and

00:05:39.789 --> 00:05:41.229
shaking them off.

00:05:41.229 --> 00:05:47.480
For unlike the Leeches of today, these blood-suckers
aren’t just parasitic — they’re predatory.

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:52.700
Leech swarms coordinate to drag prey down
to the depths, drowning whatever unfortunate

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:55.919
creature they can latch onto.

00:05:55.919 --> 00:05:59.630
Yet one species uses this to their advantage.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:04.610
Squidcadas are cuttlefish-like creatures that
flutter along on four small wings.

00:06:04.610 --> 00:06:09.850
To feed, Squidcadas have learned to ‘fish’
for Leeches by diving into the water and letting

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:11.000
one latch on.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.139
The Squidcada then flies off with the careless
parasite, feeding on them a safe distance

00:06:16.139 --> 00:06:17.139
away.

00:06:17.139 --> 00:06:21.819
It’s a remarkably dynamic behavior, and
one you can go the whole game without seeing

00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:25.639
if you move too quickly.

00:06:25.639 --> 00:06:31.090
The Drainage System eventually washes into
the Garbage Wastes: an entire ecosystem of

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:32.560
decaying junk.

00:06:32.560 --> 00:06:37.460
Yet there is life here in the form of the
inquisitive Garbage Worms.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:42.520
These long, stretchy creatures are something
of a nuisance, with their curious nature causing

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:47.220
them to steal spears from the Slugcat when
given the opportunity — often snatching

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:50.100
them directly from the Slugcat’s hand.

00:06:50.100 --> 00:06:54.610
When no spears are in reach, Garbage Worms
will stick their heads in the grime and start

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:59.560
sucking up matter, suggesting that they feed
on inorganic material.

00:06:59.560 --> 00:07:04.849
While normally passive, make a Garbage Worm
angry and they will go on the attack — flinging

00:07:04.849 --> 00:07:09.900
the Slugcat around with their heads or even
holding the unlucky creature underwater in

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.690
an attempt to drown them.

00:07:11.690 --> 00:07:16.949
Groups of Garbage Worms will even coordinate
their attacks, their eyes turning red to indicate

00:07:16.949 --> 00:07:19.650
their hostility.

00:07:19.650 --> 00:07:23.970
In the murkiest depths of the Garbage Wastes,
a true nightmare stirs.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:29.020
Hauling themselves up from the depths, the
menacing Brother Long Legs probes for their

00:07:29.020 --> 00:07:30.020
next meal.

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:35.139
Though made of a large, bulbous mass with
several ravenous tentacles extending from

00:07:35.139 --> 00:07:40.240
their core, the Brother Long Legs are flexible
enough to squeeze through tunnels.

00:07:40.240 --> 00:07:44.729
Unable to hear or see, they rely entirely
on touch to find prey.

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:50.680
And when a Brother Long Legs latches on, they
refuse to let go — eventually wearing the

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:54.810
creature out and drawing them in to an unseen
mouth.

00:07:54.810 --> 00:07:59.800
Where these monsters came from is a mystery
that can only be solved by venturing deeper

00:07:59.800 --> 00:08:03.510
into this hostile environment…

00:08:03.510 --> 00:08:07.200
The Garbage Wastes empty into the floodwaters
of the Shoreline.

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.110
Here, a drowned empire of crumbling monoliths
greets the Slugcat.

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:17.580
It’s impressive the sense of melancholy
the pixels on screen can produce.

00:08:17.580 --> 00:08:22.800
Blue Leeches swim in these waters, a coastal
variant that is larger and more aggressive

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.520
than their red counterparts, able to pull
the Slugcat under in seconds.

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.469
If you manage to avoid the leeches, there’s
plenty of other dangers here.

00:08:32.469 --> 00:08:37.140
Even the plants of the Shoreline are carnivorous
— with the terrifying Monster Kelp ready

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:40.279
to swallow you whole if you get too close.

00:08:40.279 --> 00:08:44.410
Sensitive to movement, Monster Kelp is difficult
to sneak past.

00:08:44.410 --> 00:08:49.970
In pursuing the Slugcat, these plants will
twist and contort in unexpected ways, and

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:54.350
feel particularly ‘alive’ due to their
adaptable behavior.

00:08:54.350 --> 00:08:58.779
Behind the scenes of the Monster Kelp’s
AI, each strand of kelp moves in accordance

00:08:58.779 --> 00:09:03.329
with the Slugcat’s position instead of following
a predetermined path.

00:09:03.329 --> 00:09:08.959
When paired with the game’s physics system,
the Monster Kelp feel truly unpredictable.

00:09:08.959 --> 00:09:13.640
And killer plants aren’t the only threat
below the waves.

00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:18.750
Packs of giant Salamanders pursue the Slugcat
with lethal determination.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:24.070
Able to hunt on the land as well, these amphibious
predators share the long tongues of lizards

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.140
— and seem to be a related species.

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:33.450
The only defense the Slugcat has is to swim
with all their might.

00:09:33.450 --> 00:09:39.200
For a quick boost of speed, the Slugcat can
catch a ride by grabbing onto a Jetfish.

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:44.410
These fast-moving aquatic lifeforms expel
jets of liquid to create large amounts of

00:09:44.410 --> 00:09:47.480
momentum, which can be used to rocket out
of the water.

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:52.720
On land, Jetfish aren’t always the most
graceful, but in the depths, they make exploring

00:09:52.720 --> 00:09:55.640
much easier.

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:59.170
Yet dive too deep and you’ll find monsters.

00:09:59.170 --> 00:10:04.380
Leviathans are among Rain World’s largest
and deadliest predators, able to outpace and

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:10.230
swallow a Slugcat in just a few seconds with
their powerful clamping jaws.

00:10:10.230 --> 00:10:15.890
Seemingly a mixture of metal and organic parts,
Leviathan are the first definitive proof that

00:10:15.890 --> 00:10:20.339
not all the life in Rain World is naturally
occurring…

00:10:20.339 --> 00:10:25.480
The beings who made the now-ruined structures
of Rain World — a mysterious group called

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:28.840
the Ancients — seem to have disappeared
long ago.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:34.400
Yet there is another intelligent species that
has learned to pick through their remains

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:36.640
— the Scavengers.

00:10:36.640 --> 00:10:42.900
These early tool crafters might have the most
impressive AI across all of Rain World.

00:10:42.900 --> 00:10:47.670
Scavenger behavior is insanely complex, not
just in terms of how they interact with each

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:50.980
other, but in terms of how they interact with
you.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:56.010
Though it’s easy to miss, scavengers attempt
to communicate with your player’s Slugcat

00:10:56.010 --> 00:10:58.150
through a litany of distinct gestures.

00:10:58.150 --> 00:11:02.510
If they think the Slugcat is a threat, they’ll
point their spears at them.

00:11:02.510 --> 00:11:07.320
If you move closer, they’ll try to send
a warning by raising their spears up.

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:11.360
If you persist, they won’t hesitate to stick
you full of spears.

00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:14.390
But if you play it cool, they’ll do the
same.

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:19.600
All Scavenger behaviors work on a ‘repuation’
system, where they remember the player’s

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:21.880
actions and respond accordingly.

00:11:21.880 --> 00:11:26.840
For example: sometimes you’ll come across
places called ‘tolls,’ where Scavengers

00:11:26.840 --> 00:11:29.870
won’t let you pass unless you trade them
something interesting.

00:11:29.870 --> 00:11:34.620
And they even have a custom animation to let
you know that you haven’t paid yet.

00:11:34.620 --> 00:11:39.560
But if you do trade you something valuable
like a pearl, they’ll sort of wave you through,

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:41.790
letting you know ‘you’re good.’

00:11:41.790 --> 00:11:46.950
Trading gets more complicated with Merchants:
specific Scavengers that can offer you unique

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:48.240
technology.

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:53.399
When a Scavenger wants a specific item, they’ll
kind of paw at the ground or reach out to

00:11:53.399 --> 00:11:56.209
the item to let the Slugcat know they’d
like it.

00:11:56.209 --> 00:12:01.100
And if you give them the item, they’ll drop
something in return, like a lantern you can

00:12:01.100 --> 00:12:03.250
use to light your way through the dark.

00:12:03.250 --> 00:12:09.050
Or these blue orbs called flashbangs that
release a disorienting burst to blind enemies.

00:12:09.050 --> 00:12:13.760
But if you take something from a merchant
without offering anything from return, they’ll

00:12:13.760 --> 00:12:15.100
go on the offensive.

00:12:15.100 --> 00:12:19.920
And Scavengers won’t just mindlessly chase
after the Slugcat and then give up.

00:12:19.920 --> 00:12:25.139
If your reputation gets low enough, they will
actively seek the player out, even sending

00:12:25.139 --> 00:12:28.089
hit-squads to the Slugcats general location.

00:12:28.089 --> 00:12:33.250
It all comes together to create encounters
that at their best feel like you’re interacting

00:12:33.250 --> 00:12:37.810
with something truly intelligent.

00:12:37.810 --> 00:12:43.410
With a Scavenger lantern in hand, the Slugcat
can explore the dark, decrepit wreckage of

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:48.210
the Shaded Citadel — another ruin the Ancients
left behind.

00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:53.649
Within the forgotten lower pathways, there’s
no telling what awaits.

00:12:53.649 --> 00:12:57.139
The monstrous Wolf Spiders scurry through
the dark.

00:12:57.139 --> 00:13:02.399
Like most species in the Shaded Citadel, these
spiders react dynamically to light sources,

00:13:02.399 --> 00:13:05.199
and will generally avoid your lantern.

00:13:05.199 --> 00:13:08.449
But given time, hunger overcomes their fear.

00:13:08.449 --> 00:13:13.209
And where there is one Wolf Spider, there
are invariably others.

00:13:13.209 --> 00:13:17.040
Best to keep your lantern close.

00:13:17.040 --> 00:13:21.620
But not all creatures in the Citadel are quite
so horrifying.

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:26.029
Lantern Mice are passive, bio-luminescent
rodents that hang from the ceiling with their

00:13:26.029 --> 00:13:27.480
long tails.

00:13:27.480 --> 00:13:31.610
In these shadowy caverns, they are a welcome
source of light.

00:13:31.610 --> 00:13:36.199
Lantern Mice are notably skittish, and will
run in a panic from the Slugcat if they get

00:13:36.199 --> 00:13:37.600
too close.

00:13:37.600 --> 00:13:41.380
And Lantern Mice have good reason to be nervous.

00:13:41.380 --> 00:13:45.630
Skittering on countless legs are the aggressive
Coalescipedes.

00:13:45.630 --> 00:13:50.889
When they go on the attack, these disturbing
predators resemble giant centipedes.

00:13:50.889 --> 00:13:56.590
Yet in actuality, they are made up of numerous
spiders that procedurally combine into a chain

00:13:56.590 --> 00:13:58.360
when on the attack.

00:13:58.360 --> 00:14:05.000
Because each spider is an independent AI enemy,
getting swarmed feels all the more overwhelming.

00:14:05.000 --> 00:14:09.949
The best way to defeat the Coalescipedes is
with the bright light of a flashbang, which

00:14:09.949 --> 00:14:14.100
stops the scuttling terrors in their tracks.

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:20.160
Eventually, the Shaded Citidatel gives way
to the bleak Memory Crypts.

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:25.240
Patrolling this region like nightmarish sentries
are the shadowy Miros Birds.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:30.509
Though difficult to spot in the darkness,
these creatures seem to be biomechanical — with

00:14:30.509 --> 00:14:34.140
metallic legs and scissor-like beaks.

00:14:34.140 --> 00:14:38.149
Flashbangs are the only effective deterrent,
with the bright light causing their eyes to

00:14:38.149 --> 00:14:43.079
flash different colors, allowing the Slugcat
to sneak past.

00:14:43.079 --> 00:14:49.959
Eventually, you arrive at the underside of
a massive structure engulfed in a green electrical

00:14:49.959 --> 00:14:51.009
storm.

00:14:51.009 --> 00:14:57.610
Entering the structure, the Slugcat finds
many rooms lack gravity due to localized disruptors.

00:14:57.610 --> 00:15:02.589
These zero gravity rooms can be challenging
to move through, but the more you see, the

00:15:02.589 --> 00:15:06.600
more the entire superstructure comes to resemble
a giant computer.

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:13.130
And at the heart of the supercomputer, a god-like
artificial intelligence is waiting.

00:15:13.130 --> 00:15:18.339
This synthetic lifeform is another creation
of the Ancients, and possess the peculiar

00:15:18.339 --> 00:15:20.840
name of Five Pebbles.

00:15:20.840 --> 00:15:26.070
Using their incredible power, Five Pebbles
alters the Slugcat’s brain, allowing them

00:15:26.070 --> 00:15:28.720
to understand the Ancient’s language.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:33.520
Five Pebbles dispassionately tells the Slugcat
that a form of ‘ascension’ is waiting

00:15:33.520 --> 00:15:38.710
for them if they keep heading west, before
turning the gravity back off and sending you

00:15:38.710 --> 00:15:40.759
away.

00:15:40.759 --> 00:15:46.560
Five Pebbles isn’t the only of these AIs,
called Iterators, that the Ancients created.

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:52.540
In a forgotten corner of the Shoreline, the
unusually named ‘Looks to the Moon’ wastes

00:15:52.540 --> 00:15:53.540
away.

00:15:53.540 --> 00:15:58.449
If you speak to her, she’ll explain that
AI cannot ascend in the way the Ancients or

00:15:58.449 --> 00:16:03.589
the Slugcat can — a limitation that has
driven the Iterators that still function to

00:16:03.589 --> 00:16:07.079
despair…

00:16:07.079 --> 00:16:12.840
As the Slugcat leaves the Five Pebbles supercomputer,
they need to sneak past the gargantuan Daddy

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.230
Long Legs.

00:16:14.230 --> 00:16:19.880
These oversized relatives of the Brother Long
legs can hear the Slugcats footsteps, emitting

00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:22.699
blue rings of particles when a noise is detected.

00:16:22.699 --> 00:16:27.980
There are hidden lore details that suggest
Five Pebbles is actually the one who created

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:33.980
the Long Legs — one of many horribly failed
attempts to fix a decaying world.

00:16:33.980 --> 00:16:39.199
Behind the scenes of the Long Leg’s AI,
each tentacle procedurally searches for surfaces

00:16:39.199 --> 00:16:47.810
to latch onto — creating an unsettling yet
strikingly natural pattern of movement.

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:53.519
Continuing along the underside, the Slugcat
bumps into the bizarre grappling worms.

00:16:53.519 --> 00:16:58.920
These small, tubular creatures suspend themselves
from the walls on sticky pink appendages — a

00:16:58.920 --> 00:17:01.940
trait the Slugcat can take advantage of.

00:17:01.940 --> 00:17:07.020
By grabbing onto a Worm, the Slugcat can use
them like a living grappling hook to swing

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:09.910
to new areas.

00:17:09.910 --> 00:17:15.079
To make their way to the place of ascension,
the Slugcat must climb a massive exterior

00:17:15.079 --> 00:17:17.780
surface known simply as The Wall.

00:17:17.780 --> 00:17:21.010
And this region has perils of its own.

00:17:21.010 --> 00:17:25.929
Pole Plants are cleverly hidden predators
which, at a glance, look like any other pole

00:17:25.929 --> 00:17:27.230
to be climbed on.

00:17:27.230 --> 00:17:32.820
Get too close, and the plantlike carnivore
will spring to life, flaring out its red leaves

00:17:32.820 --> 00:17:37.540
and dragging the Slugcat into its den to be
devoured.

00:17:37.540 --> 00:17:43.040
Continuing to climb, the Slugcat is ambushed
by a skilled predator known as the Dropwig,

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:45.420
which drags them back to their lair.

00:17:45.420 --> 00:17:50.590
These insects resemble large earwigs, and
are experts at catching prey.

00:17:50.590 --> 00:17:55.890
After finding a good hiding spot on the ceiling,
the Dropwig will place bait like spears beneath

00:17:55.890 --> 00:17:59.110
their perch to lure prey into an ambush.

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:04.929
This behavior is an intriguing display of
intelligence, and makes the Dropwig a cunning

00:18:04.929 --> 00:18:08.610
adversary.

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:11.630
At last, you reach the top of the wall.

00:18:11.630 --> 00:18:16.070
Above the rain clouds for the first time in
the game, it’s a rare peaceful moment...

00:18:16.070 --> 00:18:22.910
Elsewhere atop the wall, the player’s Slugcat
encounters a spiritual remnant of an Ancient

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:28.490
known as an echo, which tells you the transcendence
you’re looking for is close at hand.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:33.549
The echo says your struggles ‘can all fade
like a morning mist beneath the glory of the

00:18:33.549 --> 00:18:34.549
sun.’

00:18:34.549 --> 00:18:39.000
By this point in the game, the player has
faced so many dangers that it almost feels

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:43.130
like the echo is speaking directly to you.

00:18:43.130 --> 00:18:48.659
To reach your destination, you must first
brave the dizzying heights of the sky islands.

00:18:48.659 --> 00:18:53.920
Flying centipedes called Centiwings twist
about in the atmosphere, undaunted by the

00:18:53.920 --> 00:18:56.360
formidable altitude.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:19:00.350
Peculiar insects called Noodleflies also buzz
about in this region.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:05.520
Though typically neutral, when threatened
they extend a retractable needle-like tip

00:19:05.520 --> 00:19:09.790
from their proboscis and attempt to spear
the Slugcat clean through.

00:19:09.790 --> 00:19:16.350
With Nooodleflies around, it is wise to move
quickly.

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:22.340
The Sky Islands lead to the expansive Farm
Arrays, the second-to-last ecosystem before

00:19:22.340 --> 00:19:23.360
the end.

00:19:23.360 --> 00:19:28.750
This biosphere is dominated by Worm Grass
— hostile plant-like creatures that attempt

00:19:28.750 --> 00:19:32.760
to latch on and consume the Slugcat when it
passes by.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:38.590
The best way to cross large fields of Worm
Grass is to hitch a ride on a Rain Deer.

00:19:38.590 --> 00:19:44.020
These towering lifeforms are the tallest land
animals on Rain World, and are able to step

00:19:44.020 --> 00:19:48.610
through the carnivorous fields thanks to their
long, powerful legs.

00:19:48.610 --> 00:19:54.039
Since Rain Deer are one of the few truly peaceful
species you can encounter, there’s something

00:19:54.039 --> 00:19:59.010
soothing about their presence…

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:05.230
At last, the player’s Slugcat reaches the
dim, dilapidated train-tunnels of the Subterranean

00:20:05.230 --> 00:20:06.520
ecosystem.

00:20:06.520 --> 00:20:12.419
The final stop on this great journey, the
region seems grim at first, but eventually,

00:20:12.419 --> 00:20:18.299
the air begins to glow with a mysterious golden
shimmer, and the shapes of ethereal sea life

00:20:18.299 --> 00:20:19.929
glimmer in the dark.

00:20:19.929 --> 00:20:25.500
Some of these environments are truly spectacular
and surreal, with the game squeezing the artistic

00:20:25.500 --> 00:20:28.409
power out of every last pixel.

00:20:28.409 --> 00:20:34.290
Eventually, the Slugcat dives into a vast
sea of glowing liquid called Void Fluid, which

00:20:34.290 --> 00:20:38.940
the game hints is the substance that allowed
the Ancients to ascend.

00:20:38.940 --> 00:20:44.030
And deep within this iridescent environment,
a final creature is waiting.

00:20:44.030 --> 00:20:50.960
Coiling in the infinite expanse, the unfathomably
vast Void Worm spirals within the fluid.

00:20:50.960 --> 00:20:56.039
This creature is by far the single largest
lifeform in Rain World, and seems to call

00:20:56.039 --> 00:20:59.900
the inky black abyss below the golden sea
its home.

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:05.549
A surprisingly inquisitive lifeform, we can
get a glimpse of its surprisingly small head

00:21:05.549 --> 00:21:08.170
when it comes to investigate the Slugcat.

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:14.270
Whether or not the Void Worm is the creator
of the Void Sea or just an inhabitant is unknown,

00:21:14.270 --> 00:21:19.510
for almost everything about this cosmic creature
and the transcendence it’s home offers is

00:21:19.510 --> 00:21:21.059
left a mystery.

00:21:21.059 --> 00:21:26.970
There’s a lot of ways to interpret Rain
World’s actual ending.

00:21:26.970 --> 00:21:31.750
On the surface level, you swim into the light
surrounded by other slug cats.

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:35.320
Are these members of the family you lost at
the beginning of the game?

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:39.930
Are the endless cycles of trials you’ve
been through finally coming to a close?

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:45.549
Or is none of this even real, and the Void
Sea the Ancients believed offered transcendence

00:21:45.549 --> 00:21:48.610
really just shows you want it thinks you want?

00:21:48.610 --> 00:21:53.640
You spend the whole game trying to rise above
the ecosystem, to break out of the food web

00:21:53.640 --> 00:21:58.270
you find yourself in — but is such a thing
really possible?

00:21:58.270 --> 00:22:05.170
I won’t give away all my opinions on Rain
World’s ending, because I think part of

00:22:05.170 --> 00:22:08.140
the game’s power comes from its ability
to be interpreted.

00:22:08.140 --> 00:22:13.360
In any case, I certainly believe that Rain
World stands out thanks to its incredibly

00:22:13.360 --> 00:22:19.260
unique and detailed approach to simulating
a virtual biosphere through procedural animation

00:22:19.260 --> 00:22:21.409
and AI interactions.

00:22:21.409 --> 00:22:26.020
If you’d like to check it out for yourself,
be aware that it is a brutally challenging

00:22:26.020 --> 00:22:30.210
game that really makes you feel like you’re
on the bottom of the food chain.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:35.640
It’s so dense that I couldn’t cover everything,
so there’s lots more to uncover if you’d

00:22:35.640 --> 00:22:39.190
like to take the plunge yourself…

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:41.570
As always, thanks for watching.

00:22:41.570 --> 00:22:46.299
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:22:46.299 --> 00:22:50.980
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:52.169
See you in the next video.

